// Garden pests and integrated pest management (2026-09-26, gardening depth).
// Read by src/systems/farming/pests.rs, which carries the model and the
// reasoning; this file carries the pests, the controls, the numbers and where
// each one came from. Sources were read on 2026-09-26. The same day the
// greenhouse air became real (data/garden/humidity.ron) and three diseases
// joined the pests as rows of this model: gray mold, powdery mildew and downy
// mildew, which humid air brings on. On 2026-09-27 the two commonest
// greenhouse pests joined them, greenhouse whitefly and western flower thrips,
// with their traps and greenhouse natural enemies, and the floating row cover,
// which keeps newcomers off a bed; those sources were read that day.
//
// WHAT THE GAME TEACHES. Integrated pest management (IPM), the order every
// extension service teaches: prevent (grow in conditions the pest does not
// like, and do not replant its host in the same place), monitor (the player
// is told once when a pest appears on a grow area), then control, gentlest
// first: by hand or with water, then natural enemies, and a least-toxic spray
// last. No broad-spectrum or synthetic insecticide is in the game: UC IPM
// warns they kill the natural enemies ("Spider mites frequently become a
// problem after applying insecticides", Pest Notes: Spider Mites, below).
//
// THE MODEL, in one paragraph (pests.rs has the detail). Each grow area holds
// a PRESSURE per pest, 0 (none) to 1 (as bad as it gets). On an area with host
// crops it grows logistically, at the pest's own rate, from a slow trickle of
// arrivals: dP/dt = (r P + a)(1 - P), per garden day, where r = ln(fold) /
// fold_days. Both r and a are scaled by the area's HOST SHARE (hosts over all
// crops in the area: a mixed planting slows a pest, a monoculture of its host
// speeds it) and by the FAVOURING CONDITIONS below: every condition a crop
// does not meet multiplies that crop's share by `unfavoured_rate`. With no
// host in the area the pressure halves every `no_host_half_life_days`, which
// is what makes rotation work. On each host crop the pressure caps health at
// 100 x (1 - max_health_loss x P), and season health is what the harvest is
// scaled by, so a neglected infestation costs yield gradually and never kills.
//
// GARDEN DAYS run at the crop growth speed (10x by default, so 120 s of play),
// the same clock the crops ripen on, so a pest's life cycle keeps its real
// length against the crop's season.
//
// GAME CHOICES, said as such (numbers no source gives):
//   unfavoured_rate 0.25: a condition not met quarters that crop's part of
//     the growth and arrivals. Anchored on the one source that gives two
//     speeds: University of Minnesota Extension (Colorado potato beetle,
//     below) has larvae developing "within 10 days if average temperatures are
//     in the mid-80s degrees Fahrenheit while it can take over a month if
//     temperatures average near 60 degrees Fahrenheit", a factor of three or
//     more. It is also the factor the farming sim already uses for a field
//     crop out of season.
//   detect_at 0.05: the pressure at which the player notices the pest when
//     checking the plants. UMN (cole crops, below): "Check at least once a
//     week". Low, so the notice comes while the gentle controls still work.
//   indoor_temp_c 21: the home's grow areas are climate controlled (the crop
//     climate factor already skips them); the HVAC sim runs per room and does
//     not reach them yet, so they are taken to sit at 21 C.
//   excess_n_seasons 2: a unit holding more than two seasons of its crop's
//     nitrogen need counts as over-fed (see aphids). A fresh unit holds 1.5
//     (data/garden/nutrients.ron), so only real over-feeding trips it: a whole
//     person-day of urine poured on one small crop does.
//   arrival_per_day, no_host_half_life_days, and every control's `removes`
//     unless its note cites a figure: the sources say what works and roughly
//     how well, not by how much per visit, so these are game estimates.
//
// PEST FIELDS
//   id, name          - the pest, and its plural display name
//   indoors, outdoors - where it lives. Outdoors is an outdoor field
//                       (farming::is_field_area); everything else is indoors.
//   hosts             - data/plants.csv ids it feeds on (a test checks each)
//   favoured_by       - conditions it thrives in; each unmet one multiplies the
//                       growth by unfavoured_rate:
//                         Temperature(min_c, max_c): the area's temperature
//                           (the weather outdoors, indoor_temp_c indoors)
//                         ExcessNitrogen: the crop's unit is over-fed
//                         WaterStress: the crop is thirsty (water below 0.2)
//                         Damp: outdoors in cloudy, foggy or wet weather, or
//                           at night
//                         Season([..]): the game season is one of these
//                         Humidity(min, max): the relative humidity (0..1) of
//                           the air the crop grows in: its grow room's
//                           (data/garden/humidity.ron) indoors, the weather's
//                           outdoors (2026-09-26)
//   disease           - true for a disease (gray mold, the mildews): said as
//                       "has appeared" and listed with the pests
//   unfavoured_rate   - this pest's own factor for an unmet condition, when
//                       its sources say it spreads ONLY in its conditions
//                       (default: the file's unfavoured_rate)
//   fold, fold_days   - growth: `fold` times as many in `fold_days` days
//   arrival_per_day   - pressure arriving per garden day on a pure host stand
//   no_host_half_life_days - with no host in the area
//   max_health_loss   - the share of health (so of yield) a full infestation
//                       takes from a host crop
//   advice            - the prevention line the player is told
//
// CONTROL FIELDS
//   kind              - Cultural, Mechanical, Biological, LeastToxic: the IPM
//                       order (changing the conditions comes first). The
//                       notice lists a pest's controls in this order.
//   item              - consumed from the backpack (none: "")
//   plants_per_item   - plants one item treats
//   water_l_per_plant - litres from the home tanks per plant (hosing)
//   removes           - share of each pest's pressure one application removes
//   removes_per_day   - share removed per garden day while it lasts (a release
//                       of natural enemies), for lasts_days, ending early when
//                       the pest is gone (the predators starve or leave)
//   ends              - releases this application kills
//   protects          - share of each pest's GROWTH (and arrivals) it stops
//                       while it lasts (lasts_days): a protectant spray that
//                       keeps new infection from starting but cures nothing
//   air_changes       - air changes it gives the area's grow room at once, with
//                       the home's air (a heat-and-vent)
//   prevents          - the pests whose conditions it takes away (listed with
//                       them, though it removes none)
//   not_on            - plants.csv ids it must not be used on: refused where
//                       one grows
//   excludes          - share of each pest's ARRIVALS it keeps out while it is
//                       on (a row cover, 2026-09-27): not of the pests already
//                       under it, which go on breeding there
//   warms_day_c, warms_night_c - degrees C it warms the crops under it while
//                       it is on, in sun and otherwise (night, and indoors):
//                       the crop climate and the pests' Temperature read it
//   m2_per_item       - square metres of ground one item covers (a row cover
//                       is sized by the bed, not the plants; 0: plants_per_item)
//   needs_soil        - it lies over soil: refused on a tower or a rack
//   indoors_only      - for greenhouses: refused on an outdoor field
//   stays_without_prey - a lasting release that keeps working when its prey
//                       is gone (sticky cards; predators with their own food)
//   takes_off         - the cover (a control id) it takes off, giving its
//                       items back to the pack
//   note              - what it does and does not work on, told to the player
//
// SOURCES (cited by short name in the entries):
//   UC IPM Aphids: Flint, M.L., "Pest Notes: Aphids", UC ANR Publication 7404,
//     updated 07/2013, https://ipm.ucanr.edu/PMG/PESTNOTES/pn7404.html
//   UC IPM Spider Mites: Godfrey, L.D., "Pest Notes: Spider Mites", UC ANR
//     Publication 7405, updated 12/2011,
//     https://ipm.ucanr.edu/PMG/PESTNOTES/pn7405.html
//   Iowa State ICM: "Twospotted Spider Mites", Integrated Crop Management
//     encyclopedia, Iowa State University Extension, updated 2022,
//     https://crops.extension.iastate.edu/encyclopedia/twospotted-spider-mites
//   UW IPCM: Cullen, E., "Dry Weather Increases Risk of Twospotted Spidermite",
//     University of Wisconsin IPM, 5 July 2012, https://ipcm.wisc.edu/?p=1762
//   UMN Cole Crops: Schuh, M., Hahn, J. and Wold-Burkness, S., "Caterpillars
//     on cole crops", University of Minnesota Extension, reviewed 2022,
//     https://extension.umn.edu/yard-and-garden-insects/caterpillars-cole-crops
//   UMN VegEdge: Hutchison, W.D., Bolin, P.C. and Hines, R.L., "Imported
//     Cabbageworm", VegEdge, University of Minnesota,
//     https://vegedge.umn.edu/insect-pest-profiles/imported-cabbageworm
//   UC IPM Snails and Slugs: Wilen, C.A. and Flint, M.L., "Pest Notes: Snails
//     and Slugs", UC ANR Publication 7427, updated 03/2018,
//     https://ipm.ucanr.edu/PMG/PESTNOTES/pn7427.html
//   UMN CPB: Schuh, M., Hahn, J. and Burkness, S., "Colorado potato beetles",
//     University of Minnesota Extension, reviewed 2026,
//     https://extension.umn.edu/yard-and-garden-insects/colorado-potato-beetles
//   Cornell CPB: Martinez, L. and Nault, B., "Colorado Potato Beetle Vegetable
//     IPM Fact Sheet", Cornell Cooperative Extension, April 2025
//   UKY CPB: Bessin, R., "Colorado Potato Beetle Management", ENTFACT-312,
//     University of Kentucky, revised 1/2026,
//     https://entomology.mgcafe.uky.edu/ef312
//   CSU Soaps: Cranshaw, W.S., "Insect Control: Soaps and Detergents", Colorado
//     State University Extension fact sheet 5.547, December 1996, reviewed
//     August 2025
//   Clemson Soaps: Ubl, J.D. and Munnerlyn, C., revised by Williamson, J.,
//     "Insecticidal Soaps for Garden Pest Control", Clemson HGIC, updated
//     30 July 2021
//   Tremblay 2009: Tremblay, E., Belanger, A., Brosseau, M. and Boivin, G.,
//     "Toxicity effects of an insecticidal soap on the green peach aphid",
//     Phytoprotection 90(1): 35-39
//   UC IPM Phytoseiulus: "Phytoseiulus Predatory Mites", UC IPM Natural
//     Enemies Gallery, https://ipm.ucanr.edu/natural-enemies/phytoseiulus-predatory-mites/
//   The disease sources (added 2026-09-26, read that day):
//   UMass: Wick, R.L., revised by Madeiras, A., "Botrytis Blight of Greenhouse
//     Crops", UMass Amherst CAFE fact sheet, last updated 5 June 2019,
//     https://www.umass.edu/agriculture-food-environment/greenhouse-floriculture/fact-sheets/botrytis-blight-of-greenhouse-crops
//   UC IPM Gray Mold: "Botrytis Blight, or Gray Mold", UC IPM Home and
//     Landscape (no date on the page),
//     https://ipm.ucanr.edu/home-and-landscape/botrytis-blight-or-gray-mold/
//   OSU Gray Mold: Chin, A. and Miller, S.A., "Botrytis Gray Mold", Vegetable
//     Disease Facts, The Ohio State University (no date on the page),
//     https://u.osu.edu/vegetablediseasefacts/tomato-diseases/high-tunnel-diseases/botrytis-gray-mold/
//   Clemson Gray Mold: Doubrava, N. and Blake, J.H., revised by Williamson, J.,
//     "Gray Mold (Botrytis Blight) on Flowers, Fruits, and Vegetables", Clemson
//     HGIC, updated 6 March 2026, https://hgic.clemson.edu/factsheet/gray-mold-botrytis-blight-2/
//   PNW Gray Mold: "Tomato (Solanum lycopersicum)-Gray Mold (Ghost Spot)",
//     Pacific Northwest Plant Disease Management Handbook, revised March 2026,
//     https://pnwhandbooks.org/plantdisease/host-disease/tomato-solanum-lycopersicum-gray-mold-ghost-spot
//   UF PP152: Mertely, J.C., Oliveira, M.S. and Peres, N.A., "Botrytis Fruit
//     Rot or Gray Mold of Strawberry", UF/IFAS Extension PP152,
//     https://ask.ifas.ufl.edu/publication/PP152
//   UC IPM 7406: Davis, R.M., Gubler, W.D. and Koike, S.T., "Pest Notes:
//     Powdery Mildew on Vegetables", UC ANR Publication 7406, updated 11/2008,
//     https://ipm.ucanr.edu/home-and-landscape/powdery-mildew-on-vegetables/
//   UC IPM 7493: Downer, A.J., de la Fuente, M. and Messenger-Sikes, B., "Pest
//     Notes: Powdery Mildew on Ornamentals", UC ANR Publication 7493, updated
//     02/2026, https://ipm.ucanr.edu/home-and-landscape/powdery-mildew-on-ornamentals/
//   Cornell PM: McGrath, M.T., "Cucurbit Powdery Mildew", Cornell Vegetables
//     disease fact sheet, April 2024,
//     https://www.vegetables.cornell.edu/pest-management/disease-factsheets/cucurbit-powdery-mildew/
//   PSU Mildews: Pollard, A.C., "Addressing Downy Mildew and Powdery Mildew in
//     the Home Garden", Penn State Extension, updated 8 June 2026,
//     https://extension.psu.edu/addressing-downy-mildew-and-powdery-mildew-in-the-home-garden
//   MilStop: "MilStop SP Foliar Fungicide" (BioWorks), ARBICO Organics product
//     page (no date on the page),
//     https://www.arbico-organics.com/product/milstop-foliar-fungicide-potassium-bicarbonate/natural-organic-plant-disease-control
//   Hi-Yield Sulfur: "Hi-Yield Dusting Wettable Sulfur", label text on the
//     DoMyOwn product page (no date on the page),
//     https://www.domyown.com/hiyield-dusting-wettable-sulfur-p-1567.html
//   The whitefly, thrips and row cover sources (added 2026-09-27, every one
//   read that day):
//   UC IPM Whiteflies: Flint, M.L., "Pest Notes: Whiteflies", UC ANR
//     Publication 7401, updated 09/2015,
//     https://ipm.ucanr.edu/PMG/PESTNOTES/pn7401.html
//   UC IPM Floriculture: "Whiteflies", UC IPM Pest Management Guidelines:
//     Floriculture and Ornamental Nurseries, text updated 01/22,
//     https://ipm.ucanr.edu/agriculture/floriculture-and-ornamental-nurseries/whiteflies/
//   CSU Whitefly: Cranshaw, W.S., "Greenhouse Whitefly", Colorado State
//     University Extension fact sheet 5.587, published February 1999,
//     reviewed August 2025, https://extension.colostate.edu/resource/greenhouse-whitefly/
//   UNH Whitefly: Eaton, A., "Greenhouse Whitefly [fact sheet]", University of
//     New Hampshire Extension (no date on the page),
//     https://extension.unh.edu/resource/greenhouse-whitefly-fact-sheet
//   UMass Whiteflies: Smith, T., "Whiteflies on Greenhouse Crops", UMass
//     Amherst Extension Greenhouse Crops and Floriculture Program, 2012,
//     https://www.umass.edu/agriculture-food-environment/greenhouse-floriculture/fact-sheets/whiteflies-on-greenhouse-crops
//   UMass Biocontrol: Smith, T., "Biological Control: Greenhouse Pests and
//     their Natural Enemies", UMass Amherst Extension, revised 2015, last
//     updated 16 June 2026,
//     https://www.umass.edu/agriculture-food-environment/greenhouse-floriculture/fact-sheets/biological-control-greenhouse-pests-their-natural-enemies
//   Cornell Encarsia: Hoddle, M., "Encarsia formosa", Biological Control: A
//     Guide to Natural Enemies in North America, Cornell University (no date
//     on the page), https://biocontrol.entomology.cornell.edu/parasitoids/encarsia.php
//   NCSU Encarsia: Baker, J., "Encarsia formosa", NC State Extension,
//     published 8 March 2017, reviewed 21 December 2021,
//     https://content.ces.ncsu.edu/iencarsia-formosai
//   UC IPM Thrips: Bethke, J.A., Dreistadt, S.H. and Varela, L.G., "Pest
//     Notes: Thrips", UC ANR Publication 7429, updated 05/2014,
//     https://ipm.ucanr.edu/PMG/PESTNOTES/pn7429.html
//   UMass WFT: Smith, T., "Western Flower Thrips, Management and
//     Tospoviruses", UMass Amherst Extension, last updated 16 June 2026,
//     https://www.umass.edu/agriculture-food-environment/greenhouse-floriculture/fact-sheets/western-flower-thrips-management-tospoviruses
//   UMD Row Cover: Traunfeld, J., "Vegetable Garden Weather and Pest
//     Protection" (its section "Using row cover to protect plants"),
//     University of Maryland Extension Home and Garden Information Center,
//     March 2026, updated 25 August 2026, https://extension.umd.edu/resource/row-covers
//   UMN Cucumber Beetles: Schuh, M., Hahn, J. and Wold-Burkness, S.,
//     "Cucumber beetles", University of Minnesota Extension, reviewed 2026,
//     https://extension.umn.edu/yard-and-garden-insects/cucumber-beetles
//   UMN Flea Beetles: Schuh, M., Wold-Burkness, S., Hutchison, B., Hahn, J.
//     and Hines, R., "Flea beetles", University of Minnesota Extension,
//     reviewed 2022, https://extension.umn.edu/yard-and-garden-insects/flea-beetles
(
    unfavoured_rate: 0.25,
    detect_at: 0.05,
    indoor_temp_c: 21.0,
    excess_n_seasons: 2.0,

    pests: [
        // APHIDS. Hosts, UC IPM Aphids, table of common garden aphids:
        // "Peppers, spinach, tomato, cucurbits, carrot, lettuce, legumes, corn,
        // flowers"; "Potato, spinach, lettuce, tomato, and many others"; "Cole
        // crops and other mustard family plants". The cucurbits here are
        // cucumber, squash, zucchini, pumpkin and watermelon; the legumes are
        // the bean and pea crops; the cole crops are those UMN lists (below)
        // plus brussels sprout and bok choy (the same species as cabbage and
        // as turnip).
        // Favoured: "Many species of aphids cause the greatest damage in late
        // spring when temperatures are warm but not hot (65°-80°F)" = 18.3 to
        // 26.7 C; and "High levels of nitrogen fertilizer favor aphid
        // reproduction, so never use more nitrogen than necessary."
        // Growth: "many species of aphids can develop from newborn nymph to
        // reproducing adult in seven to eight days" and "each adult aphid can
        // produce up to 80 offspring in a matter of a week". 80-fold per
        // generation, the generation taken as 7.5 days to adulthood plus half
        // the week the 80 are born over: 11 days (r = ln 80 / 11 = 0.40 a
        // day). The standard approximation r = ln R0 / T; the 80 is UC's "up
        // to", so this is the top of the range.
        // Damage: "Low to moderate numbers of leaf-feeding aphids aren't
        // usually damaging in gardens or on trees", but "large populations can
        // turn leaves yellow and stunt shoots". max_health_loss 0.3 is a game
        // estimate for that.
        (
            id: "aphid",
            name: "Aphids",
            indoors: true,
            outdoors: true,
            hosts: [
                "pepper", "spinach", "tomato", "carrot", "lettuce", "corn", "potato",
                "cucumber", "squash", "zucchini", "pumpkin", "watermelon",
                "bean", "pea", "soybean", "fava_bean", "lentil", "chickpea", "cowpea",
                "kidney_bean", "pinto_bean", "navy_bean", "lima_bean", "runner_bean",
                "cabbage", "cauliflower", "broccoli", "kale", "rutabaga", "radish", "turnip",
                "collard", "brussels_sprout", "bok_choy", "mustard_greens",
            ],
            favoured_by: [Temperature(min_c: 18.3, max_c: 26.7), ExcessNitrogen],
            fold: 80.0,
            fold_days: 11.0,
            arrival_per_day: 0.002,
            no_host_half_life_days: 2.0,
            max_health_loss: 0.3,
            advice: "Aphids breed fastest on over-fed plants: give crops only the nitrogen they need.",
        ),
        // TWOSPOTTED SPIDER MITES. Hosts, UC IPM Spider Mites: they "feed on
        // many fruit trees, vines, berries, vegetables, and ornamental plants",
        // naming "squash, melons, and watermelons" and "sugar peas and beans";
        // Iowa State ICM and UW IPCM add soybean and corn. Strawberry stands
        // for UC's "berries" and grape for its "vines".
        // Favoured: UC, "Spider mites prefer hot, dusty conditions" and "Plants
        // under water stress also are highly susceptible"; Iowa State ICM,
        // "temperatures are greater than 85°F, humidity is less than 90
        // percent, and moisture levels are low" (85 F = 29.4 C). The humidity
        // part became a Humidity condition when the grow rooms got real air
        // (2026-09-26, data/garden/humidity.ron): below 90%.
        // Growth: Iowa State ICM, "populations can increase 70-fold in as
        // little as 6 days during the summer".
        // Damage: Iowa State ICM, "capable of reducing soybean yield by 40-60
        // percent when left untreated" (UW IPCM: "Yield reductions of 40% to
        // 60% have been documented"); UC: for squash, melons and watermelons,
        // "loss of leaves can have a significant impact on yield". 0.5 is the
        // middle of 40-60%.
        (
            id: "spider_mite",
            name: "Spider mites",
            indoors: true,
            outdoors: true,
            hosts: [
                "squash", "zucchini", "pumpkin", "watermelon", "pea", "bean", "soybean",
                "kidney_bean", "pinto_bean", "navy_bean", "lima_bean", "runner_bean",
                "corn", "strawberry", "grape",
            ],
            favoured_by: [Temperature(min_c: 29.4, max_c: 45.0), WaterStress, Humidity(min: 0.0, max: 0.90)],
            fold: 70.0,
            fold_days: 6.0,
            arrival_per_day: 0.001,
            no_host_half_life_days: 3.0,
            max_health_loss: 0.5,
            advice: "Spider mites thrive in heat and on thirsty plants: keep the crops watered.",
        ),
        // CABBAGE CATERPILLARS (imported cabbageworm and cabbage looper, one
        // entry: the same hosts, damage and controls). Outdoors only: they are
        // the larvae of a butterfly and a moth that fly in and lay eggs.
        // Hosts, UMN Cole Crops: "Cole crops include cabbage, cauliflower,
        // broccoli, kale, rutabaga, radish, turnip and collard"; UMN VegEdge
        // adds "mustard"; brussels sprout and bok choy are the same species as
        // cabbage and turnip.
        // Favoured: the growing season. UMN: imported cabbageworm adults
        // "begin to appear in gardens in mid-May" and have "3 to 5 overlapping
        // generations a year"; cabbage loopers "do not survive the winter in
        // the upper Midwest".
        // Growth: UMN VegEdge, eggs hatch "After 3 to 7 days" and "Larvae take
        // about 2 weeks to grow"; with the pupa, about 35 days a generation (3
        // to 5 in a season). fold 10 a generation is a game estimate (most
        // eggs and caterpillars are eaten or parasitized; VegEdge names
        // Cotesia, Trichogramma and Pteromalus wasps): the pest arrives more
        // than it breeds in place.
        // Damage: UMN, "Cole crops can tolerate some feeding damage", but
        // "Extensive feeding can also prevent the head formation of cabbage,
        // cauliflower and broccoli" and "Severe defoliation of young seedlings
        // and transplants can cause distorted growth or death". 0.5 is a game
        // estimate.
        (
            id: "cabbage_caterpillar",
            name: "Cabbage caterpillars",
            indoors: false,
            outdoors: true,
            hosts: [
                "cabbage", "cauliflower", "broccoli", "kale", "rutabaga", "radish", "turnip",
                "collard", "mustard_greens", "brussels_sprout", "bok_choy",
            ],
            favoured_by: [Season(["spring", "summer", "autumn"])],
            fold: 10.0,
            fold_days: 35.0,
            arrival_per_day: 0.004,
            no_host_half_life_days: 7.0,
            max_health_loss: 0.5,
            advice: "Moths and butterflies lay their eggs through the growing season; young caterpillars are the easiest to stop.",
        ),
        // SLUGS. Outdoors only. Hosts, UC IPM Snails and Slugs: "Some plants
        // these pests will seriously damage include basil, beans, cabbage,
        // dahlia, delphinium, hosta, lettuce, marigolds, strawberries, and many
        // other vegetable plants"; "they are primarily pests of seedlings and
        // herbaceous plants" and of "ripening fruits that are close to the
        // ground, such as strawberries and tomatoes".
        // Favoured: "Snails and slugs are most active at night and on cloudy or
        // foggy days"; drip rather than sprinkler irrigation "will reduce
        // humidity and moist surfaces, making the habitat less favorable".
        // Growth: "Slugs reach maturity after about 3 to 6 months" (135 days
        // taken); fold 10 is a game estimate. Slugs build up over seasons, not
        // weeks, and stay in the ground between crops (half-life 60 days, a
        // game estimate): UC advises removing the "shady, cool places where
        // they may hide".
        // Damage: "irregular holes with smooth edges on leaves and flowers";
        // 0.3 is a game estimate.
        (
            id: "slug",
            name: "Slugs",
            indoors: false,
            outdoors: true,
            hosts: [
                "basil", "bean", "cabbage", "dahlia", "lettuce", "marigold", "strawberry", "tomato",
            ],
            favoured_by: [Damp],
            fold: 10.0,
            fold_days: 135.0,
            arrival_per_day: 0.004,
            no_host_half_life_days: 60.0,
            max_health_loss: 0.3,
            advice: "Slugs feed at night and in damp weather and hide in cover by day.",
        ),
        // COLORADO POTATO BEETLE. Outdoors only. Hosts, UMN CPB: potato, and
        // "It can also feed on eggplant, tomatillo and related crops".
        // Rotation is THE cultural control, because it lives in the ground
        // where potatoes grew. Cornell CPB: "Adults survive overwinter in the
        // soil during the cold months. Overwintering locations may be in the
        // previous season's potato field or along nearby field borders", and
        // "it is more common to see them walking to crop fields"; "Rotate host
        // crops at least 0.25-0.5 miles from previous years potato fields".
        // So it stays in an area between crops (no-host half-life 30 garden
        // days, a game estimate: an empty month between two potato crops
        // leaves half of it waiting; a season of something else clears most
        // of it).
        // Favoured: warmth. UMN, larvae "complete development within 10 days
        // if average temperatures are in the mid-80s degrees Fahrenheit while
        // it can take over a month if temperatures average near 60 degrees
        // Fahrenheit": below 60 F (15.6 C) it is unfavoured.
        // Growth: UKY CPB, "This insect can go from egg to adult in as little
        // as 21 days"; UMN, females "lay clusters of 10 to 30 eggs". fold 20 a
        // generation is a game estimate.
        // Damage: Cornell, "Larvae and adults cause severe defoliation,
        // reducing tuber yield. Unmanaged infestations can lead to complete
        // defoliation"; UKY, "Plants can loss up to 30% of their foliage
        // without yield loss". 0.5 is a game estimate.
        (
            id: "colorado_potato_beetle",
            name: "Colorado potato beetles",
            indoors: false,
            outdoors: true,
            hosts: ["potato", "eggplant"],
            favoured_by: [Temperature(min_c: 15.6, max_c: 45.0)],
            fold: 20.0,
            fold_days: 21.0,
            arrival_per_day: 0.003,
            no_host_half_life_days: 30.0,
            max_health_loss: 0.5,
            advice: "They spend the winter in the soil where potatoes grew: plant something else there next (rotate) and they come up to nothing to eat.",
        ),
        // GREENHOUSE WHITEFLY (Trialeurodes vaporariorum; added 2026-09-27,
        // sources read that day). UMass Whiteflies: "There are two primary
        // species of whiteflies in greenhouses, the greenhouse whitefly
        // (Trialeurodes vaporariorum) and sweet potato whitefly". Indoors all
        // year and outdoors in the warm months: CSU Whitefly, "In summer, they
        // can quickly become established in the garden", but "It does not
        // survive outdoors in areas of freezing winters".
        // Hosts: UC IPM Whiteflies, "Very broad including most vegetables and
        // herbaceous ornamentals". Named: CSU Whitefly, "tomato, cucumber
        // lettuce, beans", "calendula, cucumber, lantana, tomato, grape,
        // ageratum, bean", its photo of an "Infestation of greenhouse
        // whiteflies on zucchini leaf", and outdoors "notably tomato, pepper,
        // and cucumber"; UNH Whitefly, "cucumber, tomato, lettuce"; Cornell
        // Encarsia, the crops the wasp is used on, "eggplant ... marigolds ...
        // and strawberry". The Phaseolus beans stand for "beans".
        // Favoured: warmth. UMass Whiteflies, "Greenhouse whitefly eggs hatch
        // in four days ... at temperatures between 65-75F" and the adults "are
        // more active at temperatures of 75F"; UC IPM Floriculture,
        // "Development from egg to adult takes about 21 to 26 days at 81°F".
        // The window is 65 F (18.3 C) to 81 F (27.2 C), the span those figures
        // are given for. Outside it the sources give no fast development, so
        // the game treats it as unfavoured (a game choice, said as such).
        // Growth: UNH Whitefly, "Females lay 150 or more eggs on the underside
        // of leaves, at a rate of about 25 per day"; UMass Whiteflies, "can
        // produce up to 200 eggs"; CSU, "400 eggs over a period of up to two
        // months". The lowest, 150, is taken. Whiteflies have males and
        // females (CSU: "Ratios of male to female whiteflies vary"), so only
        // the daughters carry the next generation: half, 75 (a game estimate
        // for an even split). The generation is 29 days: 23.5 from egg to
        // adult (the middle of UC IPM Floriculture's 21 to 26), 2.5 before
        // laying (UMass: "Adult females begin to lay eggs 2-3 days after
        // emergence") and 3, half the six days UNH's 150 eggs take at 25 a
        // day. That sits inside CSU's "a generation of the greenhouse whitefly
        // takes three to four weeks". r = ln 75 / 29 = 0.149 a day.
        // Arrivals: CSU, "Since infestations originate only from infested
        // plant materials, inspection and quarantine of new plants is
        // recommended". 0.0005 a garden day is a game estimate, half the
        // spider mites' figure, because a closed home gets them only on new
        // plants.
        // No host: UC IPM Whiteflies, "removing all host plants from the
        // greenhouse for at least 2 weeks (and assuring that no whiteflies can
        // enter from outside) may eliminate problems"; CSU, "a host-free
        // period can starve them. This might mean removing all host plants for
        // 2-3 weeks." A half-life of 2 garden days leaves under 1% after two
        // weeks (a game reading of "may eliminate").
        // Damage: UC IPM Whiteflies, "Large populations can cause leaves to
        // turn yellow, appear dry, or fall off plants" and "Nymphs cause most
        // of the damage"; CSU, "Stunting, yellowing of foliage and premature
        // leaf drop are among the symptoms of injury". No source gives a yield
        // figure. UC IPM Floriculture: "Similar to aphids and psyllids,
        // whitefly adults and nymphs suck phloem sap and excrete sticky
        // honeydew", so it takes the aphids' 0.3 (a game estimate).
        (
            id: "whitefly",
            name: "Whiteflies",
            indoors: true,
            outdoors: true,
            hosts: [
                "tomato", "cucumber", "lettuce", "pepper", "eggplant", "zucchini", "grape",
                "strawberry", "calendula", "marigold",
                "bean", "kidney_bean", "pinto_bean", "navy_bean", "lima_bean", "runner_bean",
            ],
            favoured_by: [Temperature(min_c: 18.3, max_c: 27.2)],
            fold: 75.0,
            fold_days: 29.0,
            arrival_per_day: 0.0005,
            no_host_half_life_days: 2.0,
            max_health_loss: 0.3,
            advice: "Whiteflies come in on new plants and breed all year indoors: check the undersides of new plants' leaves before they join the others. Two weeks with no host plants starves them out.",
        ),
        // WESTERN FLOWER THRIPS (Frankliniella occidentalis; added 2026-09-27,
        // sources read that day). UMass WFT: "WFT is the most prevalent species
        // of thrips in Massachusetts greenhouses". Indoors all year, and
        // outdoors: UC IPM Thrips, "Greenhouse thrips and western flower thrips
        // are two common pest species in landscapes".
        // Hosts: UC IPM Thrips, Table 1, "many herbaceous ornamentals
        // (impatiens, petunia); vegetables (cucurbits, pepper); fruits (grape,
        // strawberry); some shrubs and trees (rose, stone fruit)"; UMass WFT,
        // "marigolds", "Fava bean plants can also be used as an indicator
        // plant", and the tomato spotted wilt virus it carries "is a problem
        // mostly on vegetable plants such as tomatoes and peppers". The
        // cucurbits are cucumber, squash, zucchini, pumpkin and watermelon; the
        // stone fruit peach, plum and cherry.
        // Favoured: UC IPM Thrips, "avoid excessive applications of nitrogen
        // fertilizer, which may promote higher populations of thrips". (UMass
        // WFT also has "Thrips populations will be highest when plants are
        // flowering"; the model has no flowering condition yet, so that is
        // left out.)
        // Growth: UMass WFT, "WFT females lay 150-300 eggs during their
        // lifetime": the low end, halved for the daughters as for the
        // whitefly, 75. "The life cycle is completed in two to four weeks": 21
        // days, the middle (UC IPM Thrips: "When the weather is warm, the life
        // cycle from egg to adult may be completed in as short a time as 2
        // weeks"). r = ln 75 / 21 = 0.206 a day.
        // Arrivals: UC IPM Thrips, "Thrips can readily move long distances
        // floating with the wind or transported on infested plants". 0.001 a
        // garden day is a game estimate (the spider mites' figure).
        // No host: UMass WFT, "keep the house heated and empty for a week or
        // more between crops. Note that one week may not be enough time." A
        // half-life of 2 garden days leaves about a tenth after a week and
        // under 1% after two (a game reading).
        // Damage: UC IPM Thrips, "Thrips feeding can stunt plant growth and
        // cause damaged leaves to become papery and distorted, develop tiny
        // pale spots (stippling), and drop prematurely", and "Herbaceous
        // ornamentals, and certain vegetable crops, are more susceptible to
        // serious injury from thrips feeding and thrips-vectored viruses,
        // especially when plants are young". No source gives a yield figure;
        // 0.3 is a game estimate. The viruses they carry are not modelled.
        (
            id: "thrips",
            name: "Western flower thrips",
            indoors: true,
            outdoors: true,
            hosts: [
                "cucumber", "squash", "zucchini", "pumpkin", "watermelon", "pepper", "tomato",
                "grape", "strawberry", "rose", "peach", "plum", "cherry", "marigold", "fava_bean",
            ],
            favoured_by: [ExcessNitrogen],
            fold: 75.0,
            fold_days: 21.0,
            arrival_per_day: 0.001,
            no_host_half_life_days: 2.0,
            max_health_loss: 0.3,
            advice: "Thrips hide in flowers and buds and drop to the soil to pupate, so sprays reach few of them: check new plants, do not over-feed nitrogen, and release their predatory mites early.",
        ),

        // -- DISEASES (2026-09-26, greenhouse humidity) ------------------------
        // Rows in the same model as the pests: a pressure per grow area that
        // builds on its host crops in the conditions it likes and caps their
        // health. What drives them is the air: each has a Humidity window, the
        // grow room's relative humidity from data/garden/humidity.ron indoors
        // and the weather's outdoors. The sources say infection happens ONLY in
        // those conditions, so each carries its own `unfavoured_rate` of 0.05
        // (a GAME ESTIMATE for "only": a condition not met cuts that crop's
        // part of the growth and arrivals to a twentieth, where a pest's is a
        // quarter). Leaf wetness indoors comes from condensation, which is the
        // top of the Humidity window, and the home waters by drip and
        // aeroponics, never overhead. Every plant is sown at its cited spacing
        // (plants.csv area_per_plant_m2), so crowding is not modelled; the
        // advice says to space plants.
        // `fold` is a GAME ESTIMATE for each: tenfold a cycle, each lesion
        // making many spores of which few find a leaf in the right conditions.
        //
        // GRAY MOLD (Botrytis cinerea). Hosts: OSU Gray Mold lists "Tomato,
        // Pepper, Lettuce, Cucumber"; Clemson Gray Mold "Beans", "Tomatoes" and
        // the fruits "Apples, Blackberries, Blueberries, Grapes, Peaches,
        // Pears, Plums, Raspberries, Strawberries" (blackberries are not in
        // plants.csv).
        // Favoured: UMass, "Germination of spores and infection of the host is
        // dependent on a film of moisture for 8 to 12 hours, relative humidity
        // 85% or greater, and temperatures 55 - 75°F" (12.8 to 23.9 C). UC IPM
        // Gray Mold: "Spores germinate and produce new infections only after
        // plants have been continuously wet for 6 or more consecutive hours or
        // when relative humidity has been higher than about 90% for 6 or more
        // hours."
        // Growth: PNW Gray Mold, "The fungus will rapidly sporulate (5 to 7
        // days) on diseased tissues": 6 days a cycle.
        // Damage: UF PP152, on strawberry, "occasionally resulting in yield
        // losses greater than 50%": 0.5.
        // No host: UMass, it persists as "chlamydospores and/or microsclerotia"
        // in soil and debris; a half-life of 30 garden days is a game estimate.
        (
            id: "gray_mold",
            name: "Gray mold",
            disease: true,
            indoors: true,
            outdoors: true,
            hosts: [
                "tomato", "pepper", "lettuce", "cucumber", "bean", "strawberry", "grape",
                "blueberry", "raspberry", "apple", "peach", "pear", "plum",
            ],
            favoured_by: [Humidity(min: 0.85, max: 1.0), Temperature(min_c: 12.8, max_c: 23.9)],
            unfavoured_rate: Some(0.05),
            fold: 10.0,
            fold_days: 6.0,
            arrival_per_day: 0.002,
            no_host_half_life_days: 30.0,
            max_health_loss: 0.5,
            advice: "Gray mold infects only in air at 85% humidity or more: ventilate, space the plants, keep the leaves dry and clear away dead leaves and flowers.",
        ),
        // POWDERY MILDEW. Hosts: UC IPM 7406, "artichoke, beans, beets,
        // carrot, cucumber, eggplant, lettuce, melons, parsnips, peas, peppers,
        // pumpkins, radicchio, radishes, squash, tomatillo, tomatoes, and
        // turnips" (melons are watermelon here, radicchio is a chicory, and
        // zucchini is a summer squash; the Phaseolus beans stand for "beans").
        // Favoured: Cornell PM, "High relative humidity is favorable for
        // infection and conidial survival; however, infection can take place
        // as low as 50% RH" and "Mean temperature of 68-80°F is favorable;
        // infection can occur at 50-90°F" (10 to 32.2 C, the range it infects
        // in). Unlike the other two it needs no free water: UC IPM 7406, "all
        // powdery mildew species can germinate and infect in the absence of
        // free water", so it thrives in a greenhouse that is merely humid, and
        // ventilation alone does not stop it.
        // Growth: Cornell PM, "the length of time between infection and
        // symptom appearance is usually only 3 to 7 days, and a large number of
        // conidia can be produced in a short time": 5 days a cycle.
        // Damage: Cornell PM, "Quantity of yield is reduced due to a decrease in
        // the size or number of fruit or a decrease in the length of the
        // harvest period"; 0.3 is a game estimate.
        // No host: UC IPM 7406, "All powdery mildew fungi require living plant
        // tissue to grow": a half-life of 5 garden days is a game estimate.
        (
            id: "powdery_mildew",
            name: "Powdery mildew",
            disease: true,
            indoors: true,
            outdoors: true,
            hosts: [
                "artichoke", "bean", "kidney_bean", "pinto_bean", "navy_bean", "lima_bean", "runner_bean",
                "beet", "carrot", "cucumber", "eggplant", "lettuce", "watermelon", "parsnip", "pea",
                "pepper", "pumpkin", "chicory", "radish", "squash", "zucchini", "tomato", "turnip",
            ],
            favoured_by: [Humidity(min: 0.50, max: 1.0), Temperature(min_c: 10.0, max_c: 32.2)],
            unfavoured_rate: Some(0.05),
            fold: 10.0,
            fold_days: 5.0,
            arrival_per_day: 0.002,
            no_host_half_life_days: 5.0,
            max_health_loss: 0.3,
            advice: "Powdery mildew needs no wet leaves, only air above 50% humidity, so ventilating does little: sulfur before it spreads, or potassium bicarbonate once it has, and remove the worst leaves.",
        ),
        // DOWNY MILDEW (the cucurbit, brassica and basil downy mildews, one
        // entry: the same conditions, damage and controls). Hosts, PSU Mildews:
        // "Cucurbit downy mildew (Pseudoperonospora cubensis) – squash,
        // pumpkin, melons, cucumber"; "Downy mildew (Hyaloperonospora
        // parasitica) affects Brassicaceae: broccoli, Brussels sprouts, cabbage,
        // cauliflower, kale, radish, and turnips"; and "Basil downy mildew
        // (Peronospora belbahrii)".
        // Favoured: PSU Mildews, "Cool temperatures (58-78°F) and relative
        // humidity (>85%) near the plant or leaf surface favor infection"
        // (14.4 to 25.6 C).
        // Growth: PSU Mildews, "Post-infection, some spores begin to appear as
        // soon as four days, but 7 to 10 days is more typical": 7 days a cycle.
        // Damage: PSU Mildews, "left untreated, it can do severe damage to
        // crops and is capable of killing all plants within a week": 0.7, a
        // game estimate under the house cap (the floor keeps it from killing).
        // No host: its resting spores stay in rotting crop debris; a half-life
        // of 30 garden days is a game estimate.
        (
            id: "downy_mildew",
            name: "Downy mildew",
            disease: true,
            indoors: true,
            outdoors: true,
            hosts: [
                "squash", "zucchini", "pumpkin", "watermelon", "cucumber",
                "broccoli", "brussels_sprout", "cabbage", "cauliflower", "kale", "radish", "turnip",
                "basil",
            ],
            favoured_by: [Humidity(min: 0.85, max: 1.0), Temperature(min_c: 14.4, max_c: 25.6)],
            unfavoured_rate: Some(0.05),
            fold: 10.0,
            fold_days: 7.0,
            arrival_per_day: 0.001,
            no_host_half_life_days: 30.0,
            max_health_loss: 0.7,
            advice: "Downy mildew infects in cool air above 85% humidity: ventilate, avoid crowding and overwatering, and remove infected leaves at once.",
        ),
    ],

    controls: [
        // HOSING OFF (mechanical). UC IPM Aphids: "knock off the insects with a
        // strong spray of water. Most dislodged aphids won't be able to
        // return". UC IPM Spider Mites: "regular, forceful spraying of plants
        // with water often will reduce spider mite numbers". Half the aphids
        // and 0.4 of the mites per hosing are game estimates (mite eggs stay
        // stuck to the leaves). 0.5 L a plant is a game estimate: a few
        // seconds of a spray nozzle, drawn from the home tanks.
        // Whiteflies (2026-09-27): UC IPM Whiteflies, "Hose adults off plants
        // with a strong stream of water". Only the adults come off: "Later
        // nymphal stages are immobile, oval, and flattened", and "Nymphs cause
        // most of the damage". 0.3 is a game estimate.
        (
            id: "hose_off",
            name: "Hose off",
            kind: Mechanical,
            water_l_per_plant: 0.5,
            removes: {"aphid": 0.5, "spider_mite": 0.4, "whitefly": 0.3},
            note: "A strong spray of water knocks aphids, spider mites and adult whiteflies off; most cannot climb back. The whitefly nymphs stay stuck to the leaves, and it does nothing to caterpillars, beetles or slugs.",
        ),
        // HAND-PICKING (mechanical). UC IPM Snails and Slugs: "Hand-picking can
        // be very effective if done thoroughly on a regular basis. At first you
        // should look for snails and slugs daily." UMN Cole Crops: "Handpick
        // and drop the caterpillars into a pail of soapy water". UMN CPB:
        // "Adults and larvae can be put in a bucket of soapy water." Half per
        // picking is a game estimate: some always hide.
        (
            id: "hand_pick",
            name: "Hand-pick",
            kind: Mechanical,
            removes: {"cabbage_caterpillar": 0.5, "slug": 0.5, "colorado_potato_beetle": 0.5},
            note: "Picking them off by hand works on caterpillars, beetles and slugs if it is done often; aphids and mites are too small to pick.",
        ),
        // Bt (biological). UMN Cole Crops: "Bacillius thuringiensis (Bt) is a
        // bacterium that occurs naturally in the soil. Caterpillars must eat
        // it to be effective." UMN VegEdge: "applied when early instars are
        // present". 0.8 is a game estimate. It is the kurstaki strain, for
        // caterpillars; the beetle strain (tenebrionis, UKY CPB: "effective
        // against small larvae") is a different product, not this one.
        (
            id: "bt_spray",
            name: "Bt spray",
            kind: Biological,
            item: "bt_kurstaki_0",
            plants_per_item: 20,
            removes: {"cabbage_caterpillar": 0.8},
            note: "Bt is a soil bacterium that only caterpillars that eat it are hurt by. This strain (kurstaki) does not work on beetles, aphids, mites or slugs.",
        ),
        // PREDATORY MITES (biological), Phytoseiulus persimilis. UC IPM
        // Phytoseiulus: "Adults consume 5 to 20 prey (eggs or mites) per day",
        // "Generation times are 7 to 17 days ... twice as fast as that of its
        // prey", and "If spider mite prey are not present, these predators will
        // disperse or starve". So a release keeps working while there are
        // mites and ends when they are gone. Half the mites a day for up to 21
        // days is a game estimate for a predator that outbreeds its prey.
        // One bottle to 50 plants is a game estimate.
        (
            id: "predatory_mites",
            name: "Release predatory mites",
            kind: Biological,
            item: "predatory_mites_0",
            plants_per_item: 50,
            removes_per_day: {"spider_mite": 0.5},
            lasts_days: 21.0,
            note: "Predatory mites eat spider mites and their eggs for as long as there are any, then starve or leave. Soap sprays kill them too.",
        ),
        // INSECTICIDAL SOAP (least toxic). Made from a bar of soap and 5 L of
        // water (data/recipes.csv, mix_insecticidal_soap): about 2%, the top of
        // Clemson Soaps' "Insecticidal soaps are usually used as a 1 to 2%
        // solution". Works on: CSU Soaps, "small, soft-bodied arthropods, such
        // as aphids, young scales, whiteflies, psyllids, mealybugs, and mites".
        // Does not: "Larger insects, such as caterpillars, sawflies, and beetle
        // larvae, are generally immune to soap sprays"; slugs are not insects.
        // Contact only: "Soaps and detergents act strictly as contact
        // insecticides, with no residual effect", so it takes nothing off a
        // pest's future growth, and "repeat applications may be needed at
        // relatively short intervals (four to seven days)".
        // How much: Tremblay 2009, on green peach aphid sprayed directly: "At
        // soap concentrations higher than 12.50 g L-1, aphid mortality was
        // close to 100%, regardless of the instar". A 2% spray is about 20 g
        // per litre, so nearly every aphid it wets dies; 0.8 allows for the
        // ones under leaves it does not reach (a game estimate). Mites 0.6: the
        // eggs survive (a game estimate). Too weak does not work: Lami et al.
        // 2024 (Insects 15: 556) found a 0.5% laundry soap no better than
        // water on the same aphid.
        // It kills the predatory mites: CSU Soaps, "Both beneficial and pest
        // mites are affected." That is the thrips' cucumeris mites as well as
        // the spider mites' Phytoseiulus (2026-09-27).
        // Whiteflies (2026-09-27): UC IPM Whiteflies, "Most less-toxic products
        // such as insecticidal soaps, neem oil, or petroleum-based oils control
        // only those whiteflies that are directly sprayed" and "Be sure to
        // cover undersides of all infested leaves; usually these are the
        // lowest leaves and the most difficult to reach". 0.5 is a game
        // estimate. It spares the Encarsia wasps: NCSU Encarsia, "insecticidal
        // soap can be applied without killing immature Encarsia formosa inside
        // the parasitized whitefly nymphs".
        // Western flower thrips: UC IPM Thrips, soaps "can provide temporary
        // control, especially for greenhouse thrips", but "Most pest thrips
        // feed while hidden, often in buds and shoot tips"; UMass WFT, "The
        // eggs of WFT are well protected and in general, pesticide sprays are
        // ineffective against them", and the pupae are in the soil. 0.3 is a
        // game estimate.
        (
            id: "soap_spray",
            name: "Insecticidal soap",
            kind: LeastToxic,
            item: "insecticidal_soap_0",
            plants_per_item: 20,
            removes: {"aphid": 0.8, "spider_mite": 0.6, "whitefly": 0.5, "thrips": 0.3},
            ends: ["predatory_mites", "cucumeris_mites"],
            note: "Soap kills only the soft-bodied pests it wets (aphids, mites, whiteflies and the few thrips not hidden in the flowers), with nothing left on the leaf afterwards, so repeat it in four to seven days. Caterpillars, beetles and slugs are immune. It kills predatory mites too, but spares the Encarsia wasps inside the whitefly nymphs.",
        ),
        // IRON PHOSPHATE SLUG BAIT (least toxic). UC IPM Snails and Slugs:
        // "Iron phosphate baits ... have the advantage of being safer for use
        // around children, domestic animals, birds, fish, and other wildlife."
        // 0.6 per baiting and one 1 kg box to 50 plants are game estimates.
        (
            id: "iron_phosphate_bait",
            name: "Iron phosphate slug bait",
            kind: LeastToxic,
            item: "iron_phosphate_bait_0",
            plants_per_item: 50,
            removes: {"slug": 0.6},
            note: "Iron phosphate pellets kill the slugs that eat them and are the safest bait around children, pets and wildlife. They do nothing to insects.",
        ),

        // -- ROW COVER, TRAPS AND GREENHOUSE NATURAL ENEMIES (2026-09-27) -----
        //
        // ROW COVER (cultural: exclusion). A floating row cover: UMD Row Cover,
        // "a white, lightweight, non-woven fabric made from spun-bonded
        // polyester or polypropylene. It has a gauzy appearance and allows air,
        // sunlight, and water to easily penetrate", draped on the crop without
        // a frame (a "floating" cover). UC IPM Floriculture files it under cultural
        // control: "Exclude adult whiteflies by growing field crops beneath
        // floating row covers". It lies over soil (a bed, tray or field), never
        // a tower or a mushroom rack.
        // What it keeps out (`excludes`, a share of each pest's ARRIVALS):
        // UMD, "Animal exclusion protects against significant feeding damage
        // from pest insects, mites, rabbits, deer, birds, and groundhogs"; UC
        // IPM Aphids, "Protect seedlings with covers"; UMN Cole Crops,
        // "Floating row covers made up of lightweight all-purpose garden fabric
        // keep the adult moths from laying eggs on plants"; UMN CPB, "Row
        // covers can be placed over your potato rows to prevent Colorado potato
        // beetle from feeding and laying eggs on plants"; UC IPM Thrips, "Row
        // covers, hot caps, and other types of cages with a fine mesh can
        // exclude thrips and other insects". 0.9 of the newcomers, and 0.8 of
        // the thrips, which only the finest mesh stops (UMD, of insect netting:
        // "Mesh sizes vary, with the smallest size excluding thrips"), are game
        // estimates: never all, because pests "may enter through gaps or tears
        // in the fabric" (UMD) and "flea beetles may get under the cover
        // whenever it is lifted" (UMN Flea Beetles). Slugs live in the soil
        // under it and the diseases come with the air, so neither is listed.
        // What it cannot do is shut out what is already there, so the growth
        // in place goes on under it. UMD: pests "were already present,
        // unnoticed on plants before the row cover was applied. Some pests
        // overwinter in the soil near host plants, emerging the following
        // spring under a cover. (Examples include the adult flies of root
        // maggots, flea beetles, and Colorado potato beetles.)" UMN CPB: "Put
        // row covers in place before you see any beetles, and make sure you are
        // rotating."
        // It warms the crops under it: UMD, "In summer, temperatures under a
        // row cover can be 5 to 15°F higher than outside temperatures, leading
        // to heat stress", and the lightweight grade "gives 2°F of frost
        // protection". warms_day_c 5.6 is 10 F, the middle of 5 to 15, in sun;
        // warms_night_c 1.1 is the 2 F, at night and indoors, where no sun
        // falls on the fabric. Both the crop climate of a field and the pests'
        // Temperature conditions read it.
        // Bees cannot reach the flowers under it: UMN Cucumber Beetles, "Remove
        // it when plants start flowering. Most plants in the cucurbit family
        // require insect pollination, and row covers prevent pollinators from
        // reaching the flowers"; UMD, "Crops that don't need their flowers
        // pollinated by insects, like spring leafy greens and green beans, can
        // be kept covered until harvest." The player is told when it goes on
        // over a crop that needs bees (data/garden/pollination.ron, by:
        // Insects), and the Garden panel flags one flowering under it.
        // The fabric: UMD, "can be easily cut to size with scissors and reused
        // for 2 to 3 years", and "The useful life of row cover material is
        // typically 1 to 3 years, depending on the weight/grade, use, and
        // weather conditions ... Lightweight row covers degrade more easily
        // than heavyweight row covers". lasts_days 365 is the low end, for the
        // lightweight grade, counted in the weather: on the bed for that long
        // it is worn out. Taken off sooner, it goes back in the pack whole.
        // m2_per_item 12 is a game estimate: a piece cut 7.6 m long from a
        // 2.1 m ("7-foot wide", UMD) bolt is 16 m2 of fabric, about 12 m2 of
        // ground once the slack the crop lifts and the edges held down with
        // soil are allowed for.
        (
            id: "row_cover",
            name: "Row cover",
            kind: Cultural,
            item: "floating_row_cover_0",
            m2_per_item: 12.0,
            excludes: {"aphid": 0.9, "spider_mite": 0.9, "cabbage_caterpillar": 0.9, "colorado_potato_beetle": 0.9, "whitefly": 0.9, "thrips": 0.8},
            warms_day_c: 5.6,
            warms_night_c: 1.1,
            lasts_days: 365.0,
            needs_soil: true,
            note: "A light fabric laid over the bed and held down at the edges keeps most flying and crawling pests off: aphids, mites, whiteflies, thrips, the moths of the cabbage caterpillars and potato beetles. Lay it before they come. It shuts in any already there, and beetles that wintered in the soil come up under it, so rotate first. It warms the crops, about 6 C in summer sun, and bees cannot get under it: take it off when squash, cucumbers, melons or strawberries flower.",
        ),
        // TAKE OFF ROW COVER. UMN Cucumber Beetles: "Remove row covers when
        // plants begin to flower so pollination can occur"; UC IPM Thrips:
        // "Once plants become larger or temperatures get warmer, remove covers
        // to provide enough growing space and to prevent overheating"; UMN
        // Cole Crops: "Remove row covers after harvesting the crop". The
        // pieces go back in the pack.
        (
            id: "row_cover_off",
            name: "Take off row cover",
            kind: Cultural,
            takes_off: "row_cover",
            note: "Fold the row cover away when crops that need bees start to flower, when summer heat builds under it, or after the harvest. It goes back in your pack for the next crop.",
        ),
        // YELLOW STICKY CARDS (mechanical: trapping). UC IPM Whiteflies: "Hang
        // sticky-coated yellow traps. Use one trap for every medium-size
        // vegetable plant", and "Such traps won't eliminate damaging
        // populations but may reduce them somewhat"; CSU Whitefly: "Yellow
        // sticky traps can be used both to monitor adult whitefly populations
        // and to help suppress populations", and they "should be placed 1 per
        // 1000ft2 and replaced weekly". They catch some thrips too: UMass WFT,
        // "Blue sticky cards are more effective in detecting thrips than yellow
        // sticky cards". So a card a plant from a pack of 10 (the pack is a
        // game estimate), working for 7 garden days (replaced weekly) whether
        // or not anything is flying, and taking 0.05 of the whiteflies and
        // 0.02 of the thrips a day (game estimates for "somewhat"). They catch
        // the Encarsia wasps as well, so releasing the wasps takes them down.
        (
            id: "yellow_sticky_cards",
            name: "Yellow sticky cards",
            kind: Mechanical,
            item: "yellow_sticky_cards_0",
            plants_per_item: 10,
            removes_per_day: {"whitefly": 0.05, "thrips": 0.02},
            lasts_days: 7.0,
            stays_without_prey: true,
            note: "Adult whiteflies fly to yellow and stick. A card a plant catches some every day for a week: they are for seeing how many there are more than for control, and they catch the Encarsia wasps too.",
        ),
        // BLUE STICKY CARDS (mechanical). UMass WFT: "Blue sticky cards are more
        // effective in detecting thrips than yellow sticky cards. Therefore,
        // blue cards are best used in thrips-sensitive crops"; UC IPM Thrips:
        // "Hang blue or bright yellow sticky traps to detect flying thrips." As
        // the yellow cards: a card a plant from a pack of 10, a week, and 0.05
        // of the thrips a day (game estimates).
        (
            id: "blue_sticky_cards",
            name: "Blue sticky cards",
            kind: Mechanical,
            item: "blue_sticky_cards_0",
            plants_per_item: 10,
            removes_per_day: {"thrips": 0.05},
            lasts_days: 7.0,
            stays_without_prey: true,
            note: "Thrips are drawn to blue more than to any other colour. A card a plant catches some adults every day for a week; the larvae hidden in the flowers and the pupae in the soil are out of reach.",
        ),
        // ENCARSIA WASPS (biological), Encarsia formosa. UC IPM Floriculture:
        // "Encarsia formosa ... is highly effective for controlling greenhouse
        // whitefly. Begin any releases when greenhouse whiteflies are first
        // observed or even before and in any case before populations are high
        // ... release 2 to 5 adult parasites per plant per week". Greenhouses
        // only: UC IPM Whiteflies, "Encarsia spp. parasites are commercially
        // available for release in greenhouse situations; however, they are not
        // generally recommended for outdoor use because they are not well
        // adapted for survival in temperate zones", so it is refused on an
        // outdoor field. Slow: Cornell Encarsia, a wasp will "kill on average a
        // total of 95 nymphs over a 12 day life expectancy", and "At 21°C ...
        // the time from oviposition to adult emergence is 25 days"; UMass
        // Whiteflies, parasitized pupae "typically appear within four weeks of
        // the first release". So 0.2 of the whiteflies a day for up to 28
        // garden days (game estimates), ending early when there are none left
        // (NCSU Encarsia: "there must be a resident population of whiteflies,
        // otherwise these parasites disperse or die"). A pack of cards carrying
        // 1,000 (a game estimate) at UC's top rate of 5 a plant is 200 plants.
        // The yellow cards come down first: UMass Biocontrol, "It has been
        // advised not to use yellow sticky cards for 3-4 days following
        // release to avoid capturing adults". Soap spares them (above).
        (
            id: "encarsia",
            name: "Release Encarsia wasps",
            kind: Biological,
            item: "encarsia_formosa_0",
            plants_per_item: 200,
            removes_per_day: {"whitefly": 0.2},
            lasts_days: 28.0,
            indoors_only: true,
            ends: ["yellow_sticky_cards"],
            note: "Tiny wasps lay their eggs in whitefly nymphs, which turn black and die. They work slowly, over weeks, so release them when the first whiteflies appear. They are for greenhouses: outdoors they do not survive. Take the yellow cards down first, or they catch the wasps.",
        ),
        // CUCUMERIS MITES (biological), Neoseiulus (Amblyseius) cucumeris, in
        // slow-release sachets. UMass Biocontrol: "Western flower thrips and
        // many other thrips species may be suppressed on greenhouse crop by
        // releasing (Amblyseius (Neoseiulus) cucumeris)", but "A. cucumeris
        // feed only on the first instar nymphs so must be released early in
        // the crop cycle before thrips populations are high", and "Predatory
        // mites in sachets or small bags contain food and are intended to
        // release over several weeks". UMass WFT: they "use pollen as a food
        // source so when plants begin to flower, this can be supplemental food
        // for these mites, especially if thrips levels are low". So a release
        // works for 28 garden days (a game reading of "several weeks") whether
        // or not there are thrips (stays_without_prey), taking 0.15 of the
        // thrips a day: a game estimate, lower than the spider mites'
        // predators because these eat only the youngest larvae. Greenhouses
        // only: UC IPM Thrips, of gardens, "Releasing purchased natural
        // enemies, in most situations, is unlikely to provide satisfactory
        // thrips control". A pack of sachets to 50 plants is a game estimate.
        // Soap kills them (CSU Soaps: "Both beneficial and pest mites are
        // affected").
        (
            id: "cucumeris_mites",
            name: "Release cucumeris mites",
            kind: Biological,
            item: "cucumeris_mites_0",
            plants_per_item: 50,
            removes_per_day: {"thrips": 0.15},
            lasts_days: 28.0,
            indoors_only: true,
            stays_without_prey: true,
            note: "Sachets of predatory mites that eat young thrips larvae for weeks, living on the bran in the sachet and on pollen while there are none. They cannot kill adult thrips, so hang them early, before the thrips build up. They are for greenhouses, and a soap spray kills them.",
        ),

        // -- DISEASE CONTROLS (2026-09-26), in the IPM order -----------------
        //
        // VENTILATE (cultural: change the conditions, first). A heat-and-vent
        // of the grow room the area stands in: one air change with the home's
        // air (data/garden/humidity.ron has the arithmetic). UMass: "To prevent
        // excessive humidity and dew formation, turn on the heat and open the
        // vents. The warm humid air is ventilated to the outside as the cooler
        // outside air is drawn into the greenhouse." PSU Mildews: "Ensure good
        // air circulation by staking plants and/or selective pruning, keeping
        // the relative humidity around the plants low." It kills nothing: it
        // takes away the humidity the diseases need, for as long as it lasts,
        // which is an hour or two before the crops breathe it back. An exhaust
        // fan does it all the time.
        (
            id: "ventilate",
            name: "Ventilate",
            kind: Cultural,
            air_changes: 1.0,
            prevents: ["gray_mold", "powdery_mildew", "downy_mildew"],
            note: "Heat and vent: the humid air goes out and the home's drier air comes in. It kills nothing, it takes away the damp the diseases need, and the crops breathe it back within an hour or two. An exhaust fan does it all the time.",
        ),
        // REMOVE INFECTED LEAVES (mechanical: sanitation). UC IPM Gray Mold:
        // "Remove and dispose of old blossoms, declining green plant tissue,
        // plant debris, fallen leaves, and weeds." PSU Mildews, for downy and
        // powdery mildew: "If the symptoms of either disease are detected,
        // begin by eliminating infected leaves, branches, flowers, or fruits."
        // UMass: "Remove plant debris from the greenhouse completely; do not
        // place it in garbage cans." How much one pass removes is a game
        // estimate: half the gray mold (the rot is on dying tissue that is easy
        // to see), 0.4 of the downy mildew, 0.3 of the powdery mildew (its
        // spores are already on leaves that still look clean).
        (
            id: "remove_leaves",
            name: "Remove infected leaves",
            kind: Mechanical,
            removes: {"gray_mold": 0.5, "downy_mildew": 0.4, "powdery_mildew": 0.3},
            note: "Picking off the infected leaves, flowers and fallen debris, and carrying them out of the room, removes the spores' source. It does nothing to insects.",
        ),
        // SULFUR (least toxic, a protectant). garden_sulfur_0, the same 2 kg bag
        // of 90% sulfur the soil pH amendment uses. UC IPM 7493: "Sulfur
        // products have been used to manage powdery mildew for centuries but are
        // effective only when applied before the disease appears." Cornell PM:
        // "Sulfur is one of the most effective and least expensive products."
        // So it takes nothing off what is there: it keeps new infection from
        // starting, 0.9 of the growth (a game estimate), for 7 garden days.
        // Hi-Yield Sulfur (a 90% dusting and wettable sulfur, label text):
        // "4 tbs. per gallon of water", "Repeat applications at 7-10 day
        // intervals", and "Do not use on Cucurbits (Cucumbers, Cantaloupes,
        // Melons, Squash)" (UC IPM 7406: "sulfur can be damaging to some squash
        // and melon varieties"), so it is refused where a cucurbit grows.
        // How far a bag goes: MilStop (below) gives "1 gal covers ~550 sq. ft."
        // for a foliar spray; at 4 tablespoons (about 60 g, a game estimate for a
        // fine powder) to the gallon, 2 kg is 33 gallons over about 1,700 m2
        // of leaf, and at a quarter square metre a plant (a game estimate)
        // that is 6,800 plants.
        (
            id: "sulfur",
            name: "Sulfur",
            kind: LeastToxic,
            item: "garden_sulfur_0",
            plants_per_item: 6800,
            protects: {"powdery_mildew": 0.9},
            lasts_days: 7.0,
            not_on: ["cucumber", "squash", "zucchini", "pumpkin", "watermelon"],
            note: "Sulfur stops powdery mildew from starting for about a week but does not cure what is already there, so it goes on before the mildew spreads. Never on cucumbers, squash or melons, which it burns, and not in heat near 32 C.",
        ),
        // POTASSIUM BICARBONATE (least toxic). potassium_bicarbonate_0, a 454 g
        // jar (bought, data/trade_goods.ron). UC IPM 7493: "Several products
        // containing potassium bicarbonate such as MilStop effectively
        // eradicate powdery mildew fungi", and "Do not use bicarbonate on
        // advanced stages of powdery mildew disease as phytotoxicity will be
        // severe." MilStop, as sold: "Preventative Control: Alternaria blight,
        // Anthracnose, black spot, Botrytis blight, Cercospora leaf spot, Downy
        // Mildew, Phomopsis blight, Septoria leaf spot and more.", "For smaller
        // areas, mix at 1 tbsp per gallon of water.", "1 gal covers ~550 sq.
        // ft.", and "Each application remains effective for 1-2 weeks." So it
        // removes 0.6 of the powdery mildew (a game estimate for "effectively
        // eradicate") and guards against new gray mold and downy mildew, 0.7 of
        // their growth (a game estimate), for 7 garden days. A 454 g jar at
        // about 15 g a tablespoon is 30 gallons over about 1,550 m2 of leaf:
        // 6,000 plants at a quarter square metre each.
        (
            id: "potassium_bicarbonate",
            name: "Potassium bicarbonate",
            kind: LeastToxic,
            item: "potassium_bicarbonate_0",
            plants_per_item: 6000,
            removes: {"powdery_mildew": 0.6},
            protects: {"gray_mold": 0.7, "downy_mildew": 0.7},
            lasts_days: 7.0,
            note: "A tablespoon to a gallon of water kills the powdery mildew it wets and guards the leaves against gray mold and downy mildew for about a week. Use it early: on badly mildewed leaves it burns them.",
        ),
    ],
)
